# Task 2 — Test lab: one Ubuntu Server guest and one Windows 11 guest on one computer

## 0. Starting point: the host figures from Task 1

The lab must run on the computer I inventoried in Task 1. The figures that matter for planning are these.

| Resource | Figure from Task 1 |
|---|---|
| Machine | ASUS TUF Gaming F15 FX506HE, running Windows 11 Pro (this is the host) |
| CPU | Intel Core i5-11400H: 6 physical cores, 12 logical threads. Hardware virtualization is enabled in UEFI, so a hypervisor can run 64-bit guests with hardware assistance |
| RAM | One 8 GB DDR4-3200 module, of which about **7.9 GB is usable** by Windows. In the Task 1 output only about 0.7 GB was free, so the host was already close to its limit before any virtual machine existed |
| Disk | One Samsung 512 GB NVMe SSD. Windows reports this as about 476 GiB, because manufacturers count in decimal gigabytes and Windows counts in binary ones |

The hypervisor I plan to use is **Oracle VirtualBox 7.x**, installed on the Windows host. It is a type-2 hypervisor, which means the guests run as ordinary processes inside the host operating system and share its RAM, CPU and disk. That sharing is the whole difficulty of this task: every megabyte given to a guest is a megabyte taken away from the host. The arithmetic below would be almost identical with any other type-2 hypervisor.

## 1. Verdict

**This computer cannot run both guests as requested, with each guest at its official minimum and the host still usable.** The limiting resource is RAM. CPU and disk are not a problem. Section 3 proves this with numbers.

The task says that this is a valid answer provided it is proven and followed by the closest configuration that works. That configuration is in section 2. It runs both guests at the same time, keeps the Ubuntu guest reachable by ssh from the host with internet access, and leaves the host usable. What it gives up is that the Windows 11 guest gets 3 GB of RAM, which is **below Microsoft's 4 GB minimum**, and the host is left with only a thin safety margin of about 0.5 GB.

